//
// AN EXISTING DROPPABLE IS NOT YIELDED (`@FR-G-Own`, `@FR-H-Copy-Refuse`).  `yield e` of an
// existing value places a copy, and a type with a drop hook cannot be copied, so yielding a local
// of one is refused at compile time.  The twin, `a-fresh-droppable-is-handed-over.loft`, yields
// one built at the yield, which is handed over and compiles.
//
// @falsified-at: none — a conformance guard at a rule pairing no guard named together; every cell
//   answered as the rule says on both backends on the tree it landed on.
// CHANNEL: the expected diagnostic.
// ARMED: no instrument — the refusal is on by default.
// WITNESS: the diagnostic names the type and the cure (a value built at the yield).
// HOLDS: the refusal on both backends.

struct Dh { id: integer }
fn OpDrop(self: Dh) { }

// @EXPECT_ERROR: cannot yield an existing `Dh` here
fn keeps() -> iterator<Dh> { d = Dh { id: 1 }; yield d; d.id = 2; }
